  "answer": "DHODH-Dependent Differentiation of Pyrimidine-Salvage-Limited Residual AML with Teriflunomide\n\nHypothesis. In cytarabine-residual AML with measured insufficient pyrimidine-salvage capacity, teriflunomide will cause DHODH-dependent pyrimidine depletion, durable myeloid differentiation, and reduced leukemia-propagating capacity within a normal-progenitor-sparing exposure window. Oxidative metabolism is an interaction covariate, not an eligibility criterion: residual-cell oxidative features [S2] and DHODH-inhibition-induced differentiation [S4] establish no predictive connection. ROS-low stem-cell biology [S1] must not be conflated with residual disease [S2]. Venetoclax–azacitidine is an established comparator, not this repurposing claim [S3].\n\nDrug choice. Unverified prior knowledge identifies teriflunomide as approved for relapsing forms of multiple sclerosis, chronically administered orally, highly protein-bound, and a DHODH inhibitor; uridine salvage can bypass inhibition. These claims, brequinar’s DHODH pharmacology/AML testing, and both drugs’ systemic tolerability require verification. Approval alone implies no advantage. Before commitment, compare verified human unbound pharmacokinetics and measured marrow exposure: require unbound marrow trough/cellular DHODH IC80 ≥1 for 24 hours, confirmed by ≥80% enzyme inhibition. Compare differentiation and normal-cell margins at target-matched exposure; preference requires superior verified coverage or margin without worse systemic tolerability. Otherwise, withdraw the preference or reject exposure-inadequate teriflunomide.\n\nContext and prevalence. In a 20-donor paired pretreatment/post-cytarabine pilot, quantify de novo and salvage UMP synthesis by calibrated isotope tracing at measured marrow uridine concentrations. Define low salvage as a de novo:salvage ratio ≥4:1 plus salvage flux during brief, nonlethal orthogonal DHODH blockade ≤25% of baseline total UMP synthesis. Transporter/salvage-enzyme expression is exploratory, not an eligibility surrogate. Advance only if ≥5/20 residual samples qualify, then validate independently. Measure oxygen consumption continuously and test its response interaction. This is a pragmatic feasibility gate, not evidence of prevalence.\n\nMechanism and maturation. Include high-salvage residual and paired pretreatment controls in stromal coculture. Validate a DHODH-occupancy assay and demonstrate inhibition preceding both UTP and CTP pools falling to ≤50% of baseline at 24 hours. Maturation requires ≥20-percentage-point excess over vehicle among initially immature, genotype-confirmed leukemic cells acquiring CD11b plus either CD14 or CD15 and mature morphology by day 7, persisting seven drug-free days, with ≤10-percentage-point excess apoptosis through 72 hours. Track individual cells and score morphology blinded. Include genetic DHODH suppression, an independently validated DHODH inhibitor, and venetoclax–azacitidine. Interpret concurrent uridine rescue only after demonstrated pool restoration; a validated, catalytically active, drug-resistant DHODH provides an additional rescue if feasible.\n\nSelectivity. Use protein-adjusted, pharmacokinetically capped exposures and healthy CD34-positive cells in resting and proliferation-matched conditions. Require a normal:AML post-washout colony-inhibition EC50 ratio ≥3, allowing a justified lower bound if normal EC50 exceeds the exposure cap. At an exposure reducing AML colonies ≥50%, normal colony output and long-term multilineage engraftment must decline ≤20%. Failure of either threshold rejects the proposed selective window, even if leukemic differentiation occurs.\n\nIn vivo durability. Randomize qualifying, independent-donor cytarabine-treated patient-derived xenografts to vehicle, teriflunomide, or the validated DHODH comparator; study normal-human-CD34 xenografts in parallel. Use defined diets without artificial uridine restriction; document plasma/marrow uridine relative to patient ranges and measure marrow unbound drug, occupancy, nucleotide pools, and genotype-confirmed maturation. Correlate occupancy with phenotype, but require rescue evidence for causal attribution. After withdrawal, require ≥3-fold lower leukemia-propagating frequency in secondary limiting-dilution transplantation using equal viable leukemic-cell inputs. This separates reduced bulk burden from lost propagation.\n\nInterpretation and falsification. The speculative bridges are that limited salvage predicts sensitivity, depletion unlocks maturation rather than injury, and normal progenitors retain a margin. Donors, not wells, are statistical units. Upper 95% confidence bounds below the maturation or propagation targets despite verified inhibition and depletion falsify the mechanism–context claim. Maturation despite preemptive pool restoration contradicts the proposed mediator. Off-target mitochondrial injury, stromal effects, and proliferation differences remain alternatives; occupancy correlation alone cannot exclude them. All numerical cutoffs are prospective experimental choices, not established facts. Donor heterogeneity, culture-dependent salvage, transplantation artifacts, and unverified systemic safety limit translation. Positive findings would support further investigation, not establish clinical efficacy or justify off-label treatment.",
